<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Andrew Inglis Clark</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1848">1848</a>–<a href="/years/1907">1907</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Andrew Inglis Clark was a Tasmanian lawyer, politician and judge, and a leading author of the Australian Constitution. As attorney-general of Tasmania he introduced the Hare–Clark system of proportional representation, and in 1891 he prepared a draft constitution that formed the basis of the federal convention's work. He later served as a judge of the Supreme Court of Tasmania.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/andrew-inglis-clark.jpg" alt="Andrew Inglis Clark" width="320" height="448" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: not stated · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_Andrew_Inglis_Clark_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
